                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            there is a neighborhood
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            such that the
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_X(V)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}(V)
            of sections of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is finitely presented. On a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noetherian+scheme"} noetherian scheme
            the notions of finitely presented and coherent sheaves of
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            itself is a counterexample (not coherent while finitely presented).
          %p
            The notion of coherent sheaf behaves well on the category of noetherian schemes. On a general topological space, by a basic result of Serre, if two of the sheaves of
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short+exact+sequence"} short exact sequence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo ″
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  0\to \mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{E}'\to \mathcal{E}''\to 0
          %p
            are coherent then so is the third. All this holds even if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            is a sheaf of noncommutative
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi 𝒪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℱ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom_{\mathcal{O}}(\mathcal{E},\mathcal{F})
            in the category of sheaves of
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℰ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E},\mathcal{F}
            are coherent.
          %p
            A theorem of Serre says that the category of coherent sheaves over a projective variety of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Proj
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Proj R
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a graded commutative Noetherian ring is equivalent to the localization of the category of finitely generated graded
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          %h2#historical_note_and_definition_variants Historical note and definition variants
          %p
            First works on coherent sheaves in complex analytic geometry. Serre adapted their work to algebraic framework in his famous article
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FAC"} FAC
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/EGA"} EGA
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FAC"} FAC
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A.+Vistoli"} A. Vistoli
            commented at MathOverflow
            %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/68150"} here
            that for some categorical purposes
          %blockquote
            %p one should interpret “coherent” as meaning “quasi-coherent of finite presentation”. The notion of coherent sheaf, as defined in EGA, is not functorial, that is, pullbacks of coherent sheaves are not necessarily coherent. Hartshorne’s book defines “coherent” as “quasi-coherent and finitely generated”, but this is a useless notion when working with non-noetherian schemes.
